#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# Headless runner for the VICE testbench (vendor/VICE-testprogs).
#
#   ruby --yjit bin/testbench [filter...] [--scope PREFIX] [--exclude PREFIX]
#                             [--carts] [--drive] [--1541-testsuite]
#                             [--cia-new] [--vicii-new] [--expansions] [--ntsc]
#                             [--drean] [--vic20] [--c128c64] [--c128] [--c128-z80]
#                             [--plus4] [--c16] [--pet] [--pet-discrete]
#                             [--cbm2] [--cbm5x0]
#                             [--shards N] [--list]
#                             [--results PATH] [--resume] [--engine PATH]
#
# Runs the non-interactive PAL tests from c64-testlist.in that cover
# hardware badline models: VICII, CIA, interrupts and CPU, and the
# machine-level rows of C64 and general outside the Lorenz suite. Each process
# boots a machine once, and each test runs in a child forked from it
# (test/support/forked_boot.rb) that mounts the test's directory as device 8,
# injects the .prg and types RUN. A child that crashes or hangs gets a
# FAIL row saying so, and the next test forks from the same clean boot.
# Tests report through the $D7FF debug register ($00 pass,
# $ff fail), unless the testlist says expect:error or expect:timeout;
# screenshot tests are compared pixel-by-pixel against the bundled
# reference PNGs (pepto palette, 384x272 — the same crop the GUI uses).
# Failure artifacts go to tmp/testbench/ — the mismatched screenshot for a
# screenshot test, the text screen for an exitcode one — and the results
# log, one tab-separated "id STATUS [detail]" record per test in testlist
# order, to --results (tmp/testbench/results.txt by default).
#
# --carts runs the testlist's mountcrt rows instead, from any subtree.
# Each starts a fresh machine with the cartridge attached at power-on, the
# way VICE starts it, and runs for the testlist budget alone. A row is
# listed only when badline has a mapper for its cartridge's hardware type.
# Its id names the .crt when the row has no program of its own. A row that
# also loads a program boots with the cartridge attached, loads the program
# as the other rows do and gets their boot allowance; its id names both,
# as prg+crt.
#
# --drive runs the testlist's drive/ rows instead, and the rows of the
# subtrees above that mount a .d64. Each runs on a machine booted with a
# true 1541 on the serial bus, which needs the DOS ROM (dos1541.rom in the
# ROM path). The row's mountd64 or mountg64 image goes in the drive, and
# its program loads as the other rows' do, but nothing is mounted as
# device 8 through the LOAD trap, so every disk access goes through the
# drive. A row in LOADS_FROM_DISK (test/support/testbench_drive.rb) types LOAD and
# RUN instead, and its program loads from the disk.
#
# --1541-testsuite runs drive/1541-testsuite's two rows, the same way.
# Their budgets of 2.2G and 1.85G cycles take about twelve hours each, so
# --drive leaves them out.
#
# --cia-new runs the rows the testlist tags cia-new instead, each on a
# machine whose CIAs are 6526As. Every other row runs on the 6526.
#
# --vicii-new runs the rows the testlist tags vicii-new instead, each on a
# machine whose VIC-II is an 8565, and compares a screenshot row against
# the reference VICE keeps for the 8565 when there is one. Every other row
# runs on the 6569.
#
# --expansions runs the rows that ask for a memory expansion badline
# emulates instead, from any subtree: geo512k, a 512K GEO-RAM, plus60k
# and plus256k, the +60K and +256K RAM expansions, and reu128k to reu16m,
# an REU of that size. Each boots with the expansion fitted, as VICE
# starts it, then loads and runs its program like any other row. An REU
# row that also mounts a cartridge is left out, since badline doesn't
# share I/O 2 between the two.
#
# --ntsc runs the rows the testlist tags vicii-ntsc or vicii-ntscold
# instead, each on an NTSC machine with the VIC-II it asks for, the 6567R8
# or the 6567R56A. It combines with the options above: with --vicii-new it
# takes the NTSC rows that ask for the 8562, and compares a screenshot row
# against the 8562 reference. A screenshot is cropped to VICE's NTSC view,
# 384x247 from line 28, which runs on past the last line into lines 0-11
# of the next frame. Every other row runs on PAL.
#
# --drean runs the rows the testlist tags vicii-drean instead, each on a
# Drean C64, PAL-N with the 6572. The testlist comments its one such row
# out until a testbench can run the 6572, so a commented-out row tagged
# vicii-drean counts as a row.
#
# --vic20 runs the exitcode and screenshot rows of vic20-testlist.in
# instead, each on a PAL VIC-20 with the RAM its options ask for: none for
# vic20-unexp or no option, BLK1 for vic20-8k and every block, xvic's
# -memory all, for vic20-32k. Rows that ask for a GEO-RAM drop out. The
# program goes to the start of BASIC, as xvic's -basicload puts it, and
# RUN is typed once the machine has booted. A mountcrt row starts from
# power-on with the cartridge's ROM in its block. Tests report through
# $910F, and a failure keeps the VIC-20's 22-column text screen. A
# screenshot is xvic's view, compared with the reference around the text
# window's top left corner, as xvic-hooks.sh does. Rows of badline's own
# (Testbench::VIC20_ROWS) run the split-tests programs against dumps from
# real VIC-20s and raster-ntsc against its readme; a row tagged vic-pal
# runs only without --ntsc, and one tagged vic-ntsc only with it.
#
# --c128c64 runs a curated part of the x128c64 testlist instead, the rows
# of c64-testlist.in that VICE's Makefile keeps for x128 in C64 mode, with
# cpuport.prg swapped for cpuport128.prg. Which subtrees and rows it takes
# is written out at Testbench::C128C64_DIRS (test/support/testbench_c128.rb). Each
# runs on a C128 that powers on in C64 mode, as x128's -go64 starts it, or
# with its cartridge in: a C128DCR for cia-new's 6526As, a C128 otherwise,
# the NTSC board for vicii-ntsc. Rows that ask for the 6569 drop out. A
# screenshot row compares against the 8565's reference, or the 8562's on
# NTSC, where the program has one, as a vicii-new row does on the C64.
#
# --c128 runs the rows of the x128 testlist, c128-testlist.in, that need
# C128 mode alone (Testbench::C128_DIRS has the rule), each on a PAL C128
# that powers on in C128 mode, its Z80 booting it and handing it to the
# 8502. Programs load through the C128 KERNAL's traps, but for the rows of
# Testbench::C128_DRIVE_DIRS, which run with the disk they mount in a true
# drive: the 1571 for a .d71 or .g71, a 1541 for a .d64.
#
# --c128-z80 runs the rows of the same testlist that need the Z80, on the
# same machine: the c128/z80 programs (the per-opcode timing, zex128, OUTI
# and the page relocation) and the c64modez80 and c128modez80 programs.
#
# --plus4 runs the exitcode and screenshot rows of plus4-testlist.in instead, each on a
# PAL Plus/4 with a 1551's TCBM interface plugged in, as xplus4 starts, or
# its NTSC board for ted-ntsc, and --c16 runs the same rows on a 16K C16.
# The program goes to the start of BASIC, with BASIC's end pointers set as
# LOAD sets them, and RUN is typed once the machine has booted. A mountcrt
# row starts from power-on with the cartridge's ROM in cartridge 1's
# slots. Tests report through $FDCF, and a failure keeps the 40-column
# text screen at $0C00. A screenshot row compares the TED's picture with
# the reference from (32, 35) on, as xplus4's hook does
# (Testbench::Plus4Screenshot).
#
# --pet runs the exitcode rows of pet-testlist.in instead, each on an
# 8096 with the 50 Hz editor, as xpet's hook starts it, and again on an
# 8296, its id ending in @8296, and --pet-discrete
# runs each of them on a 2001 and on a 3032, the boards with the discrete
# video, its id ending in @2001 or @3032. The program goes to the start of
# BASIC, with BASIC's end pointers set as LOAD sets them, and RUN is typed
# once the machine has booted. Tests report through $8BFF, on every
# model, and a failure keeps the 40- or 80-column text screen at $8000.
# Screenshot rows wait for the PET's video.
#
# --cbm2 runs the exitcode rows of cbm610-testlist.in on a 610, as
# xcbm2's hook starts it. The program goes to the start of BASIC's text
# in segment 1, with BASIC's end of text and the KERNAL's end address set
# as LOAD sets them, and RUN is typed once the machine has booted. A
# mountcrt row starts from power-on with the cartridge's ROM at $2000 of
# segment 15, where the KERNAL's autostart finds it. Tests report
# through $DAFF in segment 15, and a failure keeps the 80-column text
# screen at $D000. One row isn't in the testlist: selftest/cbm610-pass.d82,
# mounted as device 8 through the traps on the KERNAL's IEEE-488
# primitives, its program loaded with LOAD"*",8 and run, and another
# CBM2/cbm2-burnin.d80, Commodore's burn-in, which runs its test for the
# row's budget and passes when its grid shows every chip OK. A screenshot
# row compares the CRTC's picture with the
# reference from (32, 35) on, as xcbm2's hook does
# (Testbench::CBM2Screenshot).
# --cbm5x0 runs cbm510-testlist.in's exitcode and screenshot rows on a
# PAL 510, as xcbm5x0's hook starts it, the same way, with BASIC's text
# in segment 0 and the 40-column text screen in the static video matrix
# at $D000. A screenshot row compares the VIC-II's picture as a C64's is
# compared (Testbench::Screenshot).
#
# --scope and --exclude bound the run to an id prefix, which is how the
# suites are split: `rake regression:testbench` scopes to VICII/ and diffs
# those rows against the tracked baseline in test/baselines/,
# `rake regression:testbench-cia` scopes to CIA/, and so on. A scope can
# name several prefixes, separated by commas, as C64/,general/ does for
# testbench-general. Filters are a
# union matched inside that scope, so a filtered run stays in one
# baseline's rows; a filter that matches nothing is an error rather than
# an empty run. A filter led by ! leaves out the tests it matches, so
# `!viavarious` runs every test but those.
#
# Tests are independent — each gets its own copy of the machine — so the run is
# forked over --shards processes (SHARDS in the environment, 4 by
# default) and the per-test records merged back into testlist order.
#
# --engine runs the tests on a Spinel build of spinel/testbench.rb, or of
# spinel/vic20_testbench.rb for --vic20, spinel/c128_testbench.rb for
# --c128c64, --c128 and --c128-z80 or spinel/plus4_testbench.rb for --plus4
# and --c16, or spinel/pet_testbench.rb for --pet and --pet-discrete, instead,
# one build process per shard, each booting a fresh machine per test. The
# build only emulates, and the rows are scored here as they are for a
# test run in process (see Testbench::Engine in test/support/testbench_engine.rb).
#
# Each row also lands in PATH.progress the moment its test finishes. The
# results file is written only when every row is in, so a run that is
# killed leaves no results, but its progress file keeps what it finished,
# and running the same command again with --resume skips those tests.

require_relative "../test/support/testbench"

exit Testbench.main(ARGV)
